ich are  digitized  which really enabled me to to do this  research  and so um kind of over the year and a  half long process  of this research i've collected a number  of resources  and thought up of a few pieces of advice  that i hope are  very useful and i can kind of go in a  bit to  what i found the strengths of these  sources were  as well as kind of where they might be  lacking and so  for the first four that i have listed  under the resources  all of these were fantastic for  accessing  old publications newspapers anarchist  magazines  books that are no longer in circulation  or that are hard to come by  but especially with the first listed  source  i found that the search terms you have  to use  or the process for finding materials is  not the most straightforward  oftentimes materials are not categorized  by their name or by their subject  and so you really have to search far  afield to find what you're looking for  but once you find it they're usually  free to access free to download  um and are incredibly helpful and then  the last two are a bit more  um niche for people that are interested  in anarchists  movements whether they're in the u.s or  somewhere else but i found that the  online anarchist library and the kate  sharply library  were fantastic for kind of giving me  both a general picture of the landscape  but also for finding sources  that i didn't even know were out there  and they have dedicated teams that  curate the content  and are happy to answer questions um or  provide  advice for you um and then in terms of  advice  the main thing that i was told by my  advisor and i highly recommend was  read broadly um because i felt pressure  initially to really decide on a topic  and once i'd found it  to kind of dig down quickly and kind of  get to the work of writing  but when i stepped back i realized that  a lot of the books that i'd read whether  they were in undergrad or through the  course of my master's program  had tidbits of information o