st  studies when applying for jobs in the  china or middle east studies fields  so basically i reworked my project from  the ground up instead of focusing  narrowly on iranian maoism i decided to  carve out you know a bit of a larger  space for myself  i noted that all or nearly all studies  of cyber iranian history started around  1972  and focus on events after 1979. i  strongly suspected for my background  reading and from a few scraps of sources  i had come across  that there were substantial connections  in earlier periods as well  so i set out to write a survey of  sino-iranian relations from the  constitutional period  to the islamic revolution from 1905 to  1979  tracing different moments of both  official and unofficial sino-iranian  interactions um from the constitutional  period  to uh interactions between uh  nationalist china under chiang kai-shek  and uh the early nationalist uh  government of rezahan reza shah in iran  uh and then moving into sort of um cold  war  uh politics of the 1950s and 1960s  uh you know this was a lot broader and i  was able to keep iranian maoism as a  chapter  later on and it freed me up to find new  topics to focus on for the earlier  period  you know at first glance you would think  that this would actually make it harder  to study given the lack of sources but  it actually ended up giving me a lot  more to work with  once i realized that there were sources  i could use outside of archives  especially for those of you working on  the late 19th early 20th centuries  newspapers travelogues memoirs are an  incredible source  for day-to-day events you know that have  gone undocumented by historians  especially for those of you  who are looking to do something similar  um you know to sign to iranian relations  perhaps to look at another  uh less commonly explored uh aspect of  china's foreign policy or relations with  the rest of the world  i mean everyone's doing global history  right now right that's what we're all  we're all