 for those of us who  are facing similar circumstances  so each presenter will speak for  approximately 10 minutes and at the end  of the session we will reconvene for a  moderated q a  if you have a question at any point and  you don't necessarily have to wait  for the q a session but please feel free  to ask your question  using the q a button at the bottom of  your screen  in total this webinar should last for  approximately 75 minutes just to give  you a heads up  and it will be recorded in case you  can't stay until the end  we will send out a link to the youtube  video in a follow-up email to all  registered participants  so let us go ahead and get started  speaking first will be michael collins  michael  is a researcher focusing on china's  global governance initiatives  he was a research associate at the  council on foreign relations and before  that  he received his m.a in chinese history  from peking university's yunjin academy  so i will turn it over to you now  michael thanks so much  let me share my screen  great so um i guess the the theme of my  presentation  is researching chinese history  flexibility and  creativity so um  to start my original idea was uh or i  i began researching on my master thesis  in the fall  of 2018 uh at the time i was living on  peking university's campus  as part of the yen ching academy program  i originally intended for my thesis to  focus on  chinese anarchists at the turn of the  20th century  i wanted to show that rather than  passively receiving  anarchism from western sources whether  it was in paris or japan  chinese anarchists were playing a very  active role  and even leading the  east asian anarchist movement that was  occurring at this period  to do this i wanted to map out chinese  anarchist networks  to demonstrate their centrality to other  east and southeast asian anarchist  movements  particularly in korea and vietnam  and i was really inspired to do this by  a few different sources but particularly  by  a 