ion  um so because this is part of like the  government propaganda right this is  or accessible material that would be  kept in a collection at hoover for  instance that kind of thing  so i do think there are inroads to the  prc you just have to kind of work really  hard it's probably harder than it was  you know five years ago but  um there are possibilities out there i  think  yeah um i also want to mention i'm sure  probably a lot of people know this but  um there are a lot of chinese foreign  ministry archive documents  um available online um some of them are  even conveniently translated into  english um i  can i don't know if one of you guys can  remember i'm blanking on the website  that  um i think it's through the hoover  institute though but they're you know  they're documents that were kind of  you know taken or scanned out of foreign  ministry archives before they closed  down and they are  a lifeline to researchers trying to  piece together what's going on right now  and the wilson center as well that was  it the wilson center that's what i was  thinking of thank you that yeah they're  a great source for stuff like that  and it's growing all the time  maybe i can also chime in on the  question of prc history  my name is denise ho and i teach modern  chinese history at yale i'm one of the  co-organizers of the series  um i think maybe uh the way i would  think about  the first part of emily's question is  like with a metaphor and then  with looking at other fields so the  metaphor is just thinking about  like the number of pixels on a screen  and i think when when people  uh look back and they're nostalgic at  kind of a golden age of research in  chinese history maybe that  in the 90s in the early aughts i think  um  people are get very excited about what  they were able to find  and um and the truth is that we're just  not in that situation anymore  so i think that the people on this panel  have pointed to  many of the different ways in which you  can s